int where $r=3$ and $\\theta =0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005246/assets/63d6e0d36e3d345561b1c4c20d7d19cb34ae6bd3b7543c042be288d8fcdb12e7.png)\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection in polar coordinates is $(3,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287041 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "93f33baf-c3b5-549a-8eb7-1ab370e9c62b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Polar Circle With an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5080187-67f0-53a4-8ee1-914cf89afe62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287043} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f534cf-326e-5654-9754-154678de1eb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7f534cf-326e-5654-9754-154678de1eb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287044} :answer-field/id #uuid "87ae06d6-6114-5725-977e-4204b006c4a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287042} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9721bd06-85b5-567e-820e-60c0e6777e8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9721bd06-85b5-567e-820e-60c0e6777e8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287046} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ceca959-9b75-53ed-b311-565fc531af71" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b54a34b9-1948-5f8e-8260-327185f8c907" :question/math-academy-id "q-325154" :question/problem "Find the **Cartesian coordinates** of the point where the polar curve $r=\\frac{13}{5}$ intersects the positive $y$-axis.\n\nThe point of intersection in Cartesian coordinates is $\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "The polar curve $r=\\frac{13}{5}$ is a circle of radius $\\frac{13}{5}$ centered at the origin. It crosses the positive $y$-axis at the point $(0,\\frac{13}{5}),$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005246/assets/86158ced718c422c9b02d620ca4544eb7d2ff0e110a04fe4b7f749160fc46649.png)\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection in Cartesian coordinates is $(0,\\frac{13}{5}\\,).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287047 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "47358248-2067-590e-80e1-5d20796434c5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Circle Given a Point on the Circumference"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de316200-1dd3-5b35-a155-836c25d932b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de316200-1dd3-5b35-a155-836c25d932b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287049} :answer-field/id #uuid "eeeeff73-0133-5f30-bddb-1b150ed00d27" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fecdf4e9-12e3-5d4e-8c67-f9e63dd592f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-325173" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005246/assets/fecfcefdf82c8ae08dd8788d2a80df0a717c04344042bb38371c1dd5df95f670.png)\n\nThe polar equation of the circle centered at the origin that passes through the point $A(2,-1)$, as shown above, is$\\,\\,r=$ \n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A circle of radius $k$ centered at the origin has the polar equation $r=k.$\n\nWe can compute the radius $k$ of our circle using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the circle is $r=\\sqrt{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "47358248-2067-590e-80e1-5d20796434c5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Circle Given a Point on the Circumference"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3683145d-66ab-5add-97ed-627261a315f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\sqrt{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287052} {:answer/id #uuid "dff9868f-ebfc-50a0-9a98-808dc3593258" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287053} {:answer/id #uuid "391d28da-a37a-5e03-98d1-85ff6db9e8f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287054} {:answer/id #uuid "cb56ba48-afb4-5ebc-aae9-99969f154023" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287055} {:answer/id #uuid "74806388-a024-5838-8825-9edf7a692c93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3683145d-66ab-5add-97ed-627261a315f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\sqrt{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287052} :answer-field/id #uuid "97d7aeb3-c4e7-5241-b6a2-72272ca06df0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0df7e4f8-ccc0-517c-b0b3-90a60a4f8ae0" :question/math-academy-id "q-86954" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005246/assets/abfda82949c8ed899902e142ffab0119b734d583b299e707270e293a24a9be1b.png)\n\nFind the polar equation of the circle centered at the origin that passes through the point $A(5,2)$, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "A circle of radius $k$ centered at the origin has the polar equation $r=k.$\n\nWe can compute the radius $k$ of our circle using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the circle is $r=\\sqrt{29}.$"}]}
